.Dd October 14, 2026
.Dt XFT_CSTR 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_strlen ,
.Nm xft_cstr_to_str
.Nd NUL-terminated string helpers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In cstr.h
.Ft t_size
.Fn xft_strlen "const char *restrict str"
.Ft t_str
.Fn xft_cstr_to_str "t_allocator allocator" "const char *cstr"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn xft_strlen
returns the number of bytes in
.Fa str
before the terminating NUL byte.
It scans in native vectors of 64, 32, 16 or 8 bytes, depending on whether the
library was built for AVX-512, AVX2, SSE2 or NEON, or no SIMD.
The first load starts at
.Fa str
and is masked so that it does not cross into the next 4 KiB page; the
following loads are aligned to the vector size.
No alignment of
.Fa str
is required.
The function is
.Ql always_inline .
.Pp
.Fn xft_cstr_to_str
copies the NUL-terminated string
.Fa cstr
into a new
.Vt t_str
allocated through
.Fa allocator .
The allocation requests
.Fn xft_strlen cstr
+ 1 bytes, aligned to the next power of two of that size.
The copy includes the terminating NUL, so
.Fa .mem
of the result is itself NUL-terminated.
The result is owned by the caller and is released with
.Fn xft_str_destroy
using the same allocator.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_strlen
returns the length of
.Fa str .
.Pp
.Fn xft_cstr_to_str
returns a
.Vt t_str
whose
.Fa .size
is the length of
.Fa cstr ,
whose
.Fa .capacity
is the size of the block returned by the allocator, and whose
.Fa .mem
holds the copy.
If the allocation fails, or if
.Fa cstr
is empty, it returns a zeroed
.Vt t_str
with
.Fa .mem
set to
.Dv nullptr ;
no memory is allocated for an empty string.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <xft.h>

void	copy_name(void)
{
	t_gpa		gpa;
	t_allocator	a;
	t_str		s;

	gpa = xft_new_gpa();
	a = xft_gpa_allocator(&gpa);
	s = xft_cstr_to_str(a, "hello");
	xft_pin_invariant(s.mem != nullptr);
	xft_pin_invariant(s.size == xft_strlen("hello"));
	xft_pin_invariant(xft_memcmp(s.mem, "hello", 6) == 0);
	xft_str_destroy(a, &s);
	xft_gpa_destroy(&gpa);
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_ctype 3 ,
.Xr xft_mem 3 ,
.Xr xft_str 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
.Fn xft_strlen
reads past the terminating NUL, up to the end of the aligned vector that
contains it.
These extra bytes never lie in a 4 KiB page that holds no byte of the
string, so the over-read cannot fault on systems with pages of 4 KiB or
larger.
The function is excluded from AddressSanitizer instrumentation for this
reason.
.Pp
An empty
.Fa cstr
and an allocation failure produce the same result from
.Fn xft_cstr_to_str .
Callers that need to tell them apart must check the length of
.Fa cstr
first.
